2026-11-28 04:44:22,468 - INFO - [MainThread] - Received claude code message: {'content': [{'tool_use_id': 'call-48352db719f349359aa5d2db48eabe12', 'content': "1\t# Editable single-view reconstruction capability library\n2\t\n3\tUse the supplied RGB as evidence, not as an orthographic plan. Select an arbitrary consistent scene scale; hidden geometry, true dimensions, and unique lighting/material decomposition are not identifiable from one image. Retain alternate plausible hypotheses. Work in Blender with separate semantic objects and editable materials. The Python module `reconstruct.py` is imported from this directory inside Blender. The procedures below are hypotheses and prior knowledge, not proven cross-scene improvements.\n4\t\n5\t## Capability Name: Joint camera, layout and semantic orientation\n6\t**Applicable Conditions:** Indoor single-view reconstruction with recognizable lines, occlusions and object landmarks.\n7\t\n8\t**Problem and Technical Hypotheses:** Camera perspective, object dimensions and room layout are coupled. A plausible object viewed from a wrong camera can appear wrong; focal length and distance can trade off. Orientation must match function: doors pivot at their frame, chairs face their work surface, and appliances expose their functional face. These are hypotheses to check against the image, not guarantees. Establish image aspect ratio and normalized 2D landmarks, including corners, wall intersections, floor contacts, silhouette extrema, and high-confidence occlusion boundaries. Record front/back and relative depth separately from assumed measurements. Identify parallel line families and possible vanishing points; avoid assuming all lines are orthogonal or camera level.\n9\t\n10\t**Executable Method:** Block out room, camera, major furniture and openings as editable low-detail objects. Choose a scale anchor (e.g. nominal door height), and fix it to remove scale ambiguity. Set camera aspect, lens, position, rotation and framing; compare projected landmarks using `projected_landmarks(scene,camera,points)` and `landmark_loss(scene,camera,points,targets)`. `points` maps landmark names to world xyz; targets map names to `(x,y,weight)` normalized from the bottom left, with nonnegative weights. Loss is mean weighted squared residual. Start with room edges and major footprints; alternate camera changes with object proportions and positions, then handle silhouettes, occlusion, semantic faces and smaller elements. Render at reference aspect with the same framing after each meaningful change. For a hinged opening place a pivot at a frame edge and rotate a leaf relative to that pivot; check the leaf remains attached to the jamb across the intended swing. Avoid treating an image-space shift alone as a valid hinge repair.\n11\t\n12\t**Verification Method:** Inspect the full render side by side or as an overlay; compare landmarks, perspective convergence, silhouette, relative size, floor contacts and foreground/background occlusion. Diagnose whether multiple objects move together in image space (camera) or just one (layout). Verify the pivot and frame in plan and in the camera view. Save the candidate and notes before and after edits. The projection loss is only a guide: it does not test occlusion or shape, and landmarks behind the camera error out. For rendered visibility use actual images or ray tests. Iterative visual comparison is recommended, not evidenced as executed here.\n13\t\n14\t**Failure Conditions and Side Effects:** Sparse or mistaken landmarks, lens distortion, cropped objects and axis assumptions can mislead. Normalized coordinates depend on render aspect and Blender's camera settings. A numeric fit is not unique and may violate hidden geometry or physical plausibility. Don't assume object intent when the evidence is ambiguous.\n15\t\n16\t**Evidence and Knowledge Origin:** Common goals and reference images supplied in this workspace; general perspective/scene-layout knowledge. No development GT or student trajectories. Independent transfer not tested.\n17\t\n18\t## Capability Name: Directional appearance and illumination separation\n19\t**Applicable Conditions:** Editable indoor surfaces with visible large-scale color, roughness and directional textures.\n20\t\n21\t**Problem and Technical Hypotheses:** A photographed surface color includes illumination, shadow, exposure and material response. Reproducing a shadow by darkening an entire material can fail elsewhere. Wood grain, floor boards and tiles have directional patterns and physical repeat scale; object-local coordinates may rotate with objects, while world coordinates may be preferable for continuous surfaces.\n22\t\n23\t**Executable Method:** Establish neutral material base colors and simple area/environment lighting before adding texture. Use the callable `make_directional_material(name,color_a,color_b,scale,roughness)`, where colors are linear RGBA, scale is three positive local-coordinate frequencies and roughness is a Principled BSDF value. It creates a new material, returning it without assigning it; append it to a mesh's material slots. Object coordinates are in the object's local space: larger frequency on an axis gives shorter variation in that direction, so elongate noise along grain by reducing the frequency along that axis. Orient the object or use explicit mapping/UVs when the intended grain differs from local axes. For regular boards or tiles build parameterized geometry or UV patterns; this noise is not a board generator. Keep visible large color regions and local pattern scale more important than node count.\n24\t\n25\t**Verification Method:** Render a neutral-lit material swatch and the full scene, checking color, direction, repeat scale, and highlight width. Temporarily hold lighting fixed while changing material, then hold material fixed while changing lighting; inspect shadows and lit surfaces separately. Check object transforms, UV orientation and seams; an unwrapped UV workflow may be needed for different faces. Compare at final image resolution. These visual checks have not been executed on the development images.\n26\t\n27\t**Failure Conditions and Side Effects:** Local coordinates and nonuniform object scale change apparent world frequency; different objects may not align. Noise is not a physically accurate grain model, and texture complexity is not proof of better resemblance. A single image cannot uniquely separate albedo from lighting; exposure and color management matter. Material creation modifies Blender data and the caller must manage assignment and cleanup.\n28\t\n29\t**Evidence and Knowledge Origin:** General shader and coordinate knowledge, prompted by the RGB appearance goals. No GT-derived shader claims or transfer results.\n30\t\n31\t## Capability Name: Support and assembly diagnostics\n32\t**Applicable Conditions:** Candidate mesh scenes with hypothesized horizontal supports and semantic assemblies.\n33\t\n34\t**Problem and Technical Hypotheses:** Loose props, disconnected components and penetrations can undermine an otherwise convincing view. A support relation is directional and differs from parenting or constraints; neither AABB overlap nor parenting proves contact. Axis-aligned bounds are a coarse screen, not surface collision geometry.\n35\t\n36\t**Executable Method:** In Blender call `support_diagnostic(child,support,tolerance)` on evaluated mesh objects, with nonnegative tolerance in scene world units. The returned dict contains vertical AABB gap and XY overlap when available. Positive gap means a gap between bounding extremes; near-zero means these extremes nearly coincide; possible penetration means vertical bounding intervals overlap beyond tolerance; unknown support covers absent XY overlap or unavailable mesh geometry. Sloped surfaces, overhangs and rotation make results especially ambiguous. Call `assembly_links(objects)` to list only explicit in-set parent and object-constraint relations; this is not a geometric connectivity test. Review semantic intent independently. Do not automatically translate an object or door: first identify intended surfaces, hinge and assembly, inspect in multiple views, then make a reversible targeted edit.\n37\t\n38\t**Verification Method:** Re-run the diagnostics after edits, inspect the full assembly from side/top and camera, and use evaluated surface proximity, normals, ray casts or collision tests where appropriate. Check intentional intersections, gaps and occlusions as well as supports. Near-zero and possible penetration require surface-level follow-up; no finding proves physical contact or structural connectivity. Check that door leaf, pivot, frame and opening remain coherent after repair.\n39\t\n40\t**Failure Conditions and Side Effects:** Axis-aligned bounds may overlap even for disjoint meshes or miss the intended local contact; tolerance is scale-dependent. Empty/non-mesh objects are unknown. Positive XY overlap does not establish support. Constraints and parenting need not represent real connections; no automatic repair is performed. Evaluation of large meshes can be costly.\n41\t\n42\t**Evidence and Knowledge Origin:** User-specified verification boundaries and general geometry knowledge. No historical student actions or GT used. Synthetic tests, if performed, are recorded outside the public library in `/app/research_evidence.json`.\n43\t", 'is_error': None}], 'uuid': '9fa249fa-b755-4536-a5ba-a5b4bf6e3112', 'parent_tool_use_id': None, 'tool_use_result': {'type': 'text', 'file': {'filePath': '/evidence/A1/public_library/SKILL.md', 'content': "# Editable single-view reconstruction capability library\n\nUse the supplied RGB as evidence, not as an orthographic plan. Select an arbitrary consistent scene scale; hidden geometry, true dimensions, and unique lighting/material decomposition are not identifiable from one image. Retain alternate plausible hypotheses. Work in Blender with separate semantic objects and editable materials. The Python module `reconstruct.py` is imported from this directory inside Blender. The procedures below are hypotheses and prior knowledge, not proven cross-scene improvements.\n\n## Capability Name: Joint camera, layout and semantic orientation\n**Applicable Conditions:** Indoor single-view reconstruction with recognizable lines, occlusions and object landmarks.\n\n**Problem and Technical Hypotheses:** Camera perspective, object dimensions and room layout are coupled. A plausible object viewed from a wrong camera can appear wrong; focal length and distance can trade off. Orientation must match function: doors pivot at their frame, chairs face their work surface, and appliances expose their functional face. These are hypotheses to check against the image, not guarantees. Establish image aspect ratio and normalized 2D landmarks, including corners, wall intersections, floor contacts, silhouette extrema, and high-confidence occlusion boundaries. Record front/back and relative depth separately from assumed measurements. Identify parallel line families and possible vanishing points; avoid assuming all lines are orthogonal or camera level.\n\n**Executable Method:** Block out room, camera, major furniture and openings as editable low-detail objects. Choose a scale anchor (e.g. nominal door height), and fix it to remove scale ambiguity. Set camera aspect, lens, position, rotation and framing; compare projected landmarks using `projected_landmarks(scene,camera,points)` and `landmark_loss(scene,camera,points,targets)`. `points` maps landmark names to world xyz; targets map names to `(x,y,weight)` normalized from the bottom left, with nonnegative weights. Loss is mean weighted squared residual. Start with room edges and major footprints; alternate camera changes with object proportions and positions, then handle silhouettes, occlusion, semantic faces and smaller elements. Render at reference aspect with the same framing after each meaningful change. For a hinged opening place a pivot at a frame edge and rotate a leaf relative to that pivot; check the leaf remains attached to the jamb across the intended swing. Avoid treating an image-space shift alone as a valid hinge repair.\n\n**Verification Method:** Inspect the full render side by side or as an overlay; compare landmarks, perspective convergence, silhouette, relative size, floor contacts and foreground/background occlusion. Diagnose whether multiple objects move together in image space (camera) or just one (layout). Verify the pivot and frame in plan and in the camera view. Save the candidate and notes before and after edits. The projection loss is only a guide: it does not test occlusion or shape, and landmarks behind the camera error out. For rendered visibility use actual images or ray tests. Iterative visual comparison is recommended, not evidenced as executed here.\n\n**Failure Conditions and Side Effects:** Sparse or mistaken landmarks, lens distortion, cropped objects and axis assumptions can mislead. Normalized coordinates depend on render aspect and Blender's camera settings. A numeric fit is not unique and may violate hidden geometry or physical plausibility. Don't assume object intent when the evidence is ambiguous.\n\n**Evidence and Knowledge Origin:** Common goals and reference images supplied in this workspace; general perspective/scene-layout knowledge. No development GT or student trajectories. Independent transfer not tested.\n\n## Capability Name: Directional appearance and illumination separation\n**Applicable Conditions:** Editable indoor surfaces with visible large-scale color, roughness and directional textures.\n\n**Problem and Technical Hypotheses:** A photographed surface color includes illumination, shadow, exposure and material response. Reproducing a shadow by darkening an entire material can fail elsewhere. Wood grain, floor boards and tiles have directional patterns and physical repeat scale; object-local coordinates may rotate with objects, while world coordinates may be preferable for continuous surfaces.\n\n**Executable Method:** Establish neutral material base colors and simple area/environment lighting before adding texture. Use the callable `make_directional_material(name,color_a,color_b,scale,roughness)`, where colors are linear RGBA, scale is three positive local-coordinate frequencies and roughness is a Principled BSDF value. It creates a new material, returning it without assigning it; append it to a mesh's material slots. Object coordinates are in the object's local space: larger frequency on an axis gives shorter variation in that direction, so elongate noise along grain by reducing the frequency along that axis. Orient the object or use explicit mapping/UVs when the intended grain differs from local axes. For regular boards or tiles build parameterized geometry or UV patterns; this noise is not a board generator. Keep visible large color regions and local pattern scale more important than node count.\n\n**Verification Method:** Render a neutral-lit material swatch and the full scene, checking color, direction, repeat scale, and highlight width. Temporarily hold lighting fixed while changing material, then hold material fixed while changing lighting; inspect shadows and lit surfaces separately. Check object transforms, UV orientation and seams; an unwrapped UV workflow may be needed for different faces. Compare at final image resolution. These visual checks have not been executed on the development images.\n\n**Failure Conditions and Side Effects:** Local coordinates and nonuniform object scale change apparent world frequency; different objects may not align. Noise is not a physically accurate grain model, and texture complexity is not proof of better resemblance. A single image cannot uniquely separate albedo from lighting; exposure and color management matter. Material creation modifies Blender data and the caller must manage assignment and cleanup.\n\n**Evidence and Knowledge Origin:** General shader and coordinate knowledge, prompted by the RGB appearance goals. No GT-derived shader claims or transfer results.\n\n## Capability Name: Support and assembly diagnostics\n**Applicable Conditions:** Candidate mesh scenes with hypothesized horizontal supports and semantic assemblies.\n\n**Problem and Technical Hypotheses:** Loose props, disconnected components and penetrations can undermine an otherwise convincing view. A support relation is directional and differs from parenting or constraints; neither AABB overlap nor parenting proves contact. Axis-aligned bounds are a coarse screen, not surface collision geometry.\n\n**Executable Method:** In Blender call `support_diagnostic(child,support,tolerance)` on evaluated mesh objects, with nonnegative tolerance in scene world units. The returned dict contains vertical AABB gap and XY overlap when available. Positive gap means a gap between bounding extremes; near-zero means these extremes nearly coincide; possible penetration means vertical bounding intervals overlap beyond tolerance; unknown support covers absent XY overlap or unavailable mesh geometry. Sloped surfaces, overhangs and rotation make results especially ambiguous. Call `assembly_links(objects)` to list only explicit in-set parent and object-constraint relations; this is not a geometric connectivity test. Review semantic intent independently. Do not automatically translate an object or door: first identify intended surfaces, hinge and assembly, inspect in multiple views, then make a reversible targeted edit.\n\n**Verification Method:** Re-run the diagnostics after edits, inspect the full assembly from side/top and camera, and use evaluated surface proximity, normals, ray casts or collision tests where appropriate. Check intentional intersections, gaps and occlusions as well as supports. Near-zero and possible penetration require surface-level follow-up; no finding proves physical contact or structural connectivity. Check that door leaf, pivot, frame and opening remain coherent after repair.\n\n**Failure Conditions and Side Effects:** Axis-aligned bounds may overlap even for disjoint meshes or miss the intended local contact; tolerance is scale-dependent. Empty/non-mesh objects are unknown. Positive XY overlap does not establish support. Constraints and parenting need not represent real connections; no automatic repair is performed. Evaluation of large meshes can be costly.\n\n**Evidence and Knowledge Origin:** User-specified verification boundaries and general geometry knowledge. No historical student actions or GT used. Synthetic tests, if performed, are recorded outside the public library in `/app/research_evidence.json`.\n", 'numLines': 43, 'startLine': 1, 'totalLines': 43}}, 'origin': None}
2026-11-28 04:44:22,474 - INFO - [MainThread] - Received claude code message: {'content': [{'tool_use_id': 'call-c9e9a50d56df47e6a16a3a967f47c60d', 'content': '1\t# Editable scene reconstruction library\n2\t\n3\tUse these units together, not as a checklist of independent optimizations. A single RGB view does not identify absolute scale, hidden geometry, or a unique camera. Start from a coherent editable blockout; prioritize composition and semantic relations over tiny gaps. Reference landmarks, visible occlusions, floor/wall junctions, dominant directions and material regions are observations; geometry, light and lens are hypotheses. Keep the original blend and candidate renders separately. All functions are in `reconstruct.py`, callable from Blender after adding `/app/library` to `sys.path`. Function coordinates are Blender world units unless stated otherwise. No transfer improvement has been independently established. Synthetic execution checks do not validate image matching, rendered appearance, collision clearance, or support in arbitrary scenes; these methods remain unvalidated for those outcomes. The selection-inspired probe is not a reconstruction or independent validation.\n4\t\n5\t## Capability Name: Joint camera, layout and articulation reasoning\n6\t**Applicable Conditions:** One RGB, a blockout and identifiable image features. **Problem and Technical Hypotheses:** Position, lens, object dimensions and rotation all change projected placement and occlusion. A larger silhouette can be due to depth, width, viewing angle or camera; a chair front/back or door hinge must remain meaningful. **Executable Method:** Record image landmarks as normalized (x,y), origin bottom left. `landmark_errors(scene,camera,[(name,world_xyz,target_xy,weight),...])` returns projected coordinates, weighted image distances and behind-camera flags. `point_camera(camera,location,target,lens)` sets a candidate. Rank visible large edges and silhouettes, but inspect occlusion and orientation separately. Change camera position/target/lens in small batches, then layout and object proportions; compare consistent full-frame renders and save each candidate. `hinge_pose(hinge,child,hinge_world,closed_vector,angle)` rotates an assembly around an authored local hinge; child must already have leaf geometry offset from the origin. Use `hinge_report` with leaf hinge and jamb points to audit the structure. Both mutate transforms; duplicate first. **Verification Method:** Compare corners, slopes, relative widths and occlusion ordering in aligned full-frame renders; view top/side for hinge, wall opening, leaf swing and intersections. Recheck full frame after each repair. **Failure Conditions and Side Effects:** Landmark matches are not visibility or silhouette tests; camera fitting is underconstrained and changing focal length changes perspective. Hinge distance does not prove collision-free motion, opening width or actual physical connection. Never enlarge or translate a leaf solely to fit a silhouette if its jamb/opening is incoherent. **Evidence and Knowledge Origin:** Camera revisions and repeated rendered articulation candidates appear in strategy trajectories; the simultaneous constraints and hinge audit are general technical hypotheses, not historically validated repairs. See external research evidence.\n7\t\n8\t## Capability Name: Material coordinates, direction and lighting separation\n9\t**Applicable Conditions:** An editable planar or volumetric surface with visible structured appearance. **Problem and Technical Hypotheses:** Large directional streaks may be material, illumination, reflections, or geometry. Different coordinate frames make a texture move or change scale under transforms. **Executable Method:** `mapped_stone(name,scale,low,high,coord_object)` returns a new Blender material with object coordinates, anisotropically scaled noise and a color ramp. Assign it explicitly to the target slot. Smaller scale along an axis stretches pattern in that coordinate frame; use a shared coordinate reference object for continuity across pieces, or the default object coordinates for a single piece. This is a controllable starting hypothesis, not a marble guarantee. Lock exposure, camera, light, color management and object geometry; first match broad color, then direction, feature scale and contrast using corresponding reference and render crops. To test illumination, render a neutral diffuse material under the same lighting and compare; retain physically plausible local lamps rather than baking their glow into all surfaces. Tune only a few variables at a time. **Verification Method:** Inspect full view and matched crops, including top-to-edge continuity and visible texture direction; check again under final lighting and at final resolution. **Failure Conditions and Side Effects:** Procedural noise can look cloudy, and screen direction depends on camera and object orientation; insufficient sampling, saturation and reflections can conceal a pattern. Node complexity alone does not demonstrate improvement. Material creation changes bpy data but never assigns it. **Evidence and Knowledge Origin:** Two historical runs replaced separate vein geometry with mapped procedural material; only one records a crop comparison, which reported weaker features. This implementation is a new simplified hypothesis, not a copied or proven method.\n10\t\n11\t## Capability Name: Support and assembly diagnostics\n12\t**Applicable Conditions:** Editable evaluated meshes and explicit plausible support objects. **Problem and Technical Hypotheses:** Floating, penetration and disconnected components can be hidden in the main view. Support and attachment are distinct relations. **Executable Method:** `support_probe(obj,support_objects,tolerance,samples,reach)` samples downward rays from above evaluated lowest vertices against only the supplied candidate meshes. It returns per-point signed vertical gaps: positive gap, near-zero, possible penetration, or unknown support; mixed samples remain mixed. Select tolerance and reach relative to scene scale. `aabb_relation(a,b)` returns evaluated world AABB separations and overlap as broad-phase triage for assembly pairs. **Verification Method:** Diagnose expected pairs (leg/floor, caster/fork, stem/seat, door/jamb), inspect side and top views and perform mesh-level proximity/intersection checks as appropriate; after changing geometry rerun probes and rerender the main camera. **Failure Conditions and Side Effects:** Near-zero or AABB overlap is not proof of contact, load bearing, connectivity or absence of intersection. Rays can miss narrow geometry, hit an overhanging or upper surface, or miss supports beyond reach; low vertices may not sample curved footprints. Negative gaps indicate only possible penetration. Unknown support must not be reported as zero. Read-only diagnostics do not repair geometry. **Evidence and Knowledge Origin:** Historical caster height measurements, floor ray checks and side views motivate the diagnostics; their measurements do not prove physical contact. The broader sampled probe and interpretation limits are new technical design.\n13\t', 'is_error': None}], 'uuid': '2ee1e1dc-f8fb-489a-a225-c100ce67ac9b', 'parent_tool_use_id': None, 'tool_use_result': {'type': 'text', 'file': {'filePath': '/evidence/A2/public_library/SKILL.md', 'content': '# Editable scene reconstruction library\n\nUse these units together, not as a checklist of independent optimizations. A single RGB view does not identify absolute scale, hidden geometry, or a unique camera. Start from a coherent editable blockout; prioritize composition and semantic relations over tiny gaps. Reference landmarks, visible occlusions, floor/wall junctions, dominant directions and material regions are observations; geometry, light and lens are hypotheses. Keep the original blend and candidate renders separately. All functions are in `reconstruct.py`, callable from Blender after adding `/app/library` to `sys.path`. Function coordinates are Blender world units unless stated otherwise. No transfer improvement has been independently established. Synthetic execution checks do not validate image matching, rendered appearance, collision clearance, or support in arbitrary scenes; these methods remain unvalidated for those outcomes. The selection-inspired probe is not a reconstruction or independent validation.\n\n## Capability Name: Joint camera, layout and articulation reasoning\n**Applicable Conditions:** One RGB, a blockout and identifiable image features. **Problem and Technical Hypotheses:** Position, lens, object dimensions and rotation all change projected placement and occlusion. A larger silhouette can be due to depth, width, viewing angle or camera; a chair front/back or door hinge must remain meaningful. **Executable Method:** Record image landmarks as normalized (x,y), origin bottom left. `landmark_errors(scene,camera,[(name,world_xyz,target_xy,weight),...])` returns projected coordinates, weighted image distances and behind-camera flags. `point_camera(camera,location,target,lens)` sets a candidate. Rank visible large edges and silhouettes, but inspect occlusion and orientation separately. Change camera position/target/lens in small batches, then layout and object proportions; compare consistent full-frame renders and save each candidate. `hinge_pose(hinge,child,hinge_world,closed_vector,angle)` rotates an assembly around an authored local hinge; child must already have leaf geometry offset from the origin. Use `hinge_report` with leaf hinge and jamb points to audit the structure. Both mutate transforms; duplicate first. **Verification Method:** Compare corners, slopes, relative widths and occlusion ordering in aligned full-frame renders; view top/side for hinge, wall opening, leaf swing and intersections. Recheck full frame after each repair. **Failure Conditions and Side Effects:** Landmark matches are not visibility or silhouette tests; camera fitting is underconstrained and changing focal length changes perspective. Hinge distance does not prove collision-free motion, opening width or actual physical connection. Never enlarge or translate a leaf solely to fit a silhouette if its jamb/opening is incoherent. **Evidence and Knowledge Origin:** Camera revisions and repeated rendered articulation candidates appear in strategy trajectories; the simultaneous constraints and hinge audit are general technical hypotheses, not historically validated repairs. See external research evidence.\n\n## Capability Name: Material coordinates, direction and lighting separation\n**Applicable Conditions:** An editable planar or volumetric surface with visible structured appearance. **Problem and Technical Hypotheses:** Large directional streaks may be material, illumination, reflections, or geometry. Different coordinate frames make a texture move or change scale under transforms. **Executable Method:** `mapped_stone(name,scale,low,high,coord_object)` returns a new Blender material with object coordinates, anisotropically scaled noise and a color ramp. Assign it explicitly to the target slot. Smaller scale along an axis stretches pattern in that coordinate frame; use a shared coordinate reference object for continuity across pieces, or the default object coordinates for a single piece. This is a controllable starting hypothesis, not a marble guarantee. Lock exposure, camera, light, color management and object geometry; first match broad color, then direction, feature scale and contrast using corresponding reference and render crops. To test illumination, render a neutral diffuse material under the same lighting and compare; retain physically plausible local lamps rather than baking their glow into all surfaces. Tune only a few variables at a time. **Verification Method:** Inspect full view and matched crops, including top-to-edge continuity and visible texture direction; check again under final lighting and at final resolution. **Failure Conditions and Side Effects:** Procedural noise can look cloudy, and screen direction depends on camera and object orientation; insufficient sampling, saturation and reflections can conceal a pattern. Node complexity alone does not demonstrate improvement. Material creation changes bpy data but never assigns it. **Evidence and Knowledge Origin:** Two historical runs replaced separate vein geometry with mapped procedural material; only one records a crop comparison, which reported weaker features. This implementation is a new simplified hypothesis, not a copied or proven method.\n\n## Capability Name: Support and assembly diagnostics\n**Applicable Conditions:** Editable evaluated meshes and explicit plausible support objects. **Problem and Technical Hypotheses:** Floating, penetration and disconnected components can be hidden in the main view. Support and attachment are distinct relations. **Executable Method:** `support_probe(obj,support_objects,tolerance,samples,reach)` samples downward rays from above evaluated lowest vertices against only the supplied candidate meshes. It returns per-point signed vertical gaps: positive gap, near-zero, possible penetration, or unknown support; mixed samples remain mixed. Select tolerance and reach relative to scene scale. `aabb_relation(a,b)` returns evaluated world AABB separations and overlap as broad-phase triage for assembly pairs. **Verification Method:** Diagnose expected pairs (leg/floor, caster/fork, stem/seat, door/jamb), inspect side and top views and perform mesh-level proximity/intersection checks as appropriate; after changing geometry rerun probes and rerender the main camera. **Failure Conditions and Side Effects:** Near-zero or AABB overlap is not proof of contact, load bearing, connectivity or absence of intersection. Rays can miss narrow geometry, hit an overhanging or upper surface, or miss supports beyond reach; low vertices may not sample curved footprints. Negative gaps indicate only possible penetration. Unknown support must not be reported as zero. Read-only diagnostics do not repair geometry. **Evidence and Knowledge Origin:** Historical caster height measurements, floor ray checks and side views motivate the diagnostics; their measurements do not prove physical contact. The broader sampled probe and interpretation limits are new technical design.\n', 'numLines': 13, 'startLine': 1, 'totalLines': 13}}, 'origin': None}
2026-11-28 04:44:22,479 - INFO - [MainThread] - Received claude code message: {'content': [{'tool_use_id': 'call-13a6131b93284beeaf5cdffab0713db2', 'content': "1\t# Editable reconstruction capability library\n2\t\n3\tUse the reference as a set of image constraints, not a unique 3D truth. Establish a scale convention; hidden geometry, absolute scale, and lighting/material decomposition are ambiguous. Iterate with a fixed camera before jointly adjusting camera and layout. Keep candidate source, renders, settings, and decisions; never silently overwrite the baseline. See capabilities.json for callable modules. These methods are hypotheses and have not been independently validated on unseen scenes. Limited Blender 5.2.2 synthetic checks exercised the callable functions and a simplified selection-inspired assembly; they do not establish visual accuracy, collision-free motion, or physical contact. Image-texture and alternate-light comparisons remain unvalidated in this library.\n4\t\n5\t## Capability Name: Camera-layout and articulated occlusion reasoning\n6\t**Applicable Conditions:** A large object, doorway, furniture, or wall has the wrong silhouette, depth order, or visibility.\n7\t**Problem and Technical Hypotheses:** An edge-on face, intervening wall, incorrect hinge, or camera projection can each hide the same object. Image evidence includes visible contour, floor occlusion, wall junctions, semantic orientation, and neighboring landmarks; it does not identify the hidden room. \n8\t**Executable Method:** `spatial.project_points(scene, camera, points)` returns normalized camera projections and camera-space depth, using Blender's world-to-camera projection. `spatial.hinge_positions(hinge, width, angles, local_direction)` provides candidate free-edge locations in world XY. Specify radians and world units. Build a leaf and its details as children of a pivot at the jamb; the opening must be an actual opening, not a dark patch over solid wall. Compare swings at fixed camera first; then if necessary vary camera pose and focal length together with room dimensions. Rank reference landmarks, visible face width, relative scale, edges and occlusion, not one contour alone. Keep furniture positions coherent and parent leaf details to the hinge.\n9\t**Verification Method:** Render camera comparisons with identical lighting; temporarily hide only the suspected wall, then restore it. Inspect top and side views of *the selected* assembly, including the full swing arc, hinge to jamb alignment, opening, walls, furniture, and floor. Check projected points for positive camera depth and inspect clipping. Validate after any camera or layout revision.\n10\t**Failure Conditions and Side Effects:** Projection ignores occlusion and lens distortion; hinge helper assumes a vertical axis. Better visible leaf width alone does not justify a displaced hinge or a leaf wider than its opening. Isolated or obstructed views cannot prove absence of intersections. Single-view matching can compromise other landmarks.\n11\t**Evidence and Knowledge Origin:** General projective geometry and articulation prior; historical door trials in both repetitions motivate visibility isolation and candidate comparison. One selected case had an obstructed side diagnostic, so coherence was not established; do not treat that placement as a repair recipe. Details in research evidence.\n12\t\n13\t## Capability Name: Material mapping versus illumination experiment\n14\t**Applicable Conditions:** A surface appears too uniform, too streaky, or wrongly oriented.\n15\t**Problem and Technical Hypotheses:** Surface color, roughness, illumination, and geometry can produce similar marks. Coordinate choice and object scale change the apparent direction and wavelength; diffuse variation should remain attached to the surface when the light moves, while specular highlights can move.\n16\t**Executable Method:** `appearance.directional_stone(name, scale, colors, roughness, distortion)` creates an editable procedural Blender material using object coordinates, axis scaling, a noise texture and a color ramp; returns the material. Scale is a three-vector in local coordinate units, and colors are linear RGBA. Assign explicitly to the intended material slot. Adjust scale/axes for streak direction; apply or account for object transforms. For another candidate use an independently authored UV/image mapping with appropriate color space and packing, and a roughness-only candidate. Compare all under identical camera, exposure, geometry and render settings, then move a light independently. Do not assume object coordinates are meters after object scaling; seams and texture direction on edges need inspection.\n17\t**Verification Method:** Compare reference-aligned crops and full-frame renders for spatial frequency, contrast, coverage, edge continuity, orientation and neighboring brightness; compare each candidate under both light directions. Use neutral light and color management consistently. Record what moved and what did not; change one factor at a time.\n18\t**Failure Conditions and Side Effects:** A procedural node network or image texture is not evidence of improvement. Highlights and indirect light also depend on roughness, geometry and camera; light tests do not uniquely decompose appearance. Object-space mapping may distort on nonuniform scale; image coordinates may stretch or clamp on edges.\n19\t**Evidence and Knowledge Origin:** Both historical material runs rendered color, image and roughness candidates under alternate lighting and retained image candidates. Procedural implementation here is an independently written general hypothesis, not their selected texture or a proven transfer.\n20\t\n21\t## Capability Name: Support and assembly diagnostics\n22\t**Applicable Conditions:** An object may float, penetrate a support, or disconnect after a repair.\n23\t**Problem and Technical Hypotheses:** Bounding boxes conflate support with proximity and do not show true contact. An assembly can be connected internally yet float, or rest on a floor while components are disconnected.\n24\t**Executable Method:** `support.sample_support(obj, supports, tolerance, band, ray_margin)` samples evaluated mesh vertices in a low-Z band, casting vertically from above each sample onto the supplied evaluated mesh supports. Returns per-sample signed vertical gaps and a conservative category. World Z is up; supports must be explicitly supplied. A negative gap is only *possible* penetration. Missing rays yield unknown; near-zero is not proof of physical contact. `support.world_bounds(obj)` provides evaluated bounds for broad-phase checking only. Move a complete assembly or redesign its feet as separate candidates rather than blindly snapping individual parts.\n25\t**Verification Method:** Inspect all feet/wheels, full sample coverage, local supports, side views, clearance, and changed silhouette; repeat after the selected repair. Separately inspect joints (wheel/fork, fork/spoke, post/seat), including top/side views and evaluated geometry. Use denser surface sampling or mesh intersection testing for critical results; rays and vertices alone can miss intersections, overhangs, or unsupported areas.\n26\t**Failure Conditions and Side Effects:** Downward ray tests assume an appropriate horizontal support and do not establish stability, exact contact, connectivity, or volume intersection. The highest hit can be a top surface above the object; ray margin must exceed geometry thickness. Modifiers and sparse meshes affect sampling. Unknown is not a pass.\n27\t**Evidence and Knowledge Origin:** Historical chair runs compared whole-chair lowering against caster changes with evaluated wheel samples and side views. Their numerical checks are sampled estimates, not a physics proof; prior knowledge supplies the interpretation boundaries.\n28\t", 'is_error': None}], 'uuid': '603c7224-4f61-4935-a881-35ed61aae752', 'parent_tool_use_id': None, 'tool_use_result': {'type': 'text', 'file': {'filePath': '/evidence/A3/public_library/SKILL.md', 'content': "# Editable reconstruction capability library\n\nUse the reference as a set of image constraints, not a unique 3D truth. Establish a scale convention; hidden geometry, absolute scale, and lighting/material decomposition are ambiguous. Iterate with a fixed camera before jointly adjusting camera and layout. Keep candidate source, renders, settings, and decisions; never silently overwrite the baseline. See capabilities.json for callable modules. These methods are hypotheses and have not been independently validated on unseen scenes. Limited Blender 5.2.2 synthetic checks exercised the callable functions and a simplified selection-inspired assembly; they do not establish visual accuracy, collision-free motion, or physical contact. Image-texture and alternate-light comparisons remain unvalidated in this library.\n\n## Capability Name: Camera-layout and articulated occlusion reasoning\n**Applicable Conditions:** A large object, doorway, furniture, or wall has the wrong silhouette, depth order, or visibility.\n**Problem and Technical Hypotheses:** An edge-on face, intervening wall, incorrect hinge, or camera projection can each hide the same object. Image evidence includes visible contour, floor occlusion, wall junctions, semantic orientation, and neighboring landmarks; it does not identify the hidden room. \n**Executable Method:** `spatial.project_points(scene, camera, points)` returns normalized camera projections and camera-space depth, using Blender's world-to-camera projection. `spatial.hinge_positions(hinge, width, angles, local_direction)` provides candidate free-edge locations in world XY. Specify radians and world units. Build a leaf and its details as children of a pivot at the jamb; the opening must be an actual opening, not a dark patch over solid wall. Compare swings at fixed camera first; then if necessary vary camera pose and focal length together with room dimensions. Rank reference landmarks, visible face width, relative scale, edges and occlusion, not one contour alone. Keep furniture positions coherent and parent leaf details to the hinge.\n**Verification Method:** Render camera comparisons with identical lighting; temporarily hide only the suspected wall, then restore it. Inspect top and side views of *the selected* assembly, including the full swing arc, hinge to jamb alignment, opening, walls, furniture, and floor. Check projected points for positive camera depth and inspect clipping. Validate after any camera or layout revision.\n**Failure Conditions and Side Effects:** Projection ignores occlusion and lens distortion; hinge helper assumes a vertical axis. Better visible leaf width alone does not justify a displaced hinge or a leaf wider than its opening. Isolated or obstructed views cannot prove absence of intersections. Single-view matching can compromise other landmarks.\n**Evidence and Knowledge Origin:** General projective geometry and articulation prior; historical door trials in both repetitions motivate visibility isolation and candidate comparison. One selected case had an obstructed side diagnostic, so coherence was not established; do not treat that placement as a repair recipe. Details in research evidence.\n\n## Capability Name: Material mapping versus illumination experiment\n**Applicable Conditions:** A surface appears too uniform, too streaky, or wrongly oriented.\n**Problem and Technical Hypotheses:** Surface color, roughness, illumination, and geometry can produce similar marks. Coordinate choice and object scale change the apparent direction and wavelength; diffuse variation should remain attached to the surface when the light moves, while specular highlights can move.\n**Executable Method:** `appearance.directional_stone(name, scale, colors, roughness, distortion)` creates an editable procedural Blender material using object coordinates, axis scaling, a noise texture and a color ramp; returns the material. Scale is a three-vector in local coordinate units, and colors are linear RGBA. Assign explicitly to the intended material slot. Adjust scale/axes for streak direction; apply or account for object transforms. For another candidate use an independently authored UV/image mapping with appropriate color space and packing, and a roughness-only candidate. Compare all under identical camera, exposure, geometry and render settings, then move a light independently. Do not assume object coordinates are meters after object scaling; seams and texture direction on edges need inspection.\n**Verification Method:** Compare reference-aligned crops and full-frame renders for spatial frequency, contrast, coverage, edge continuity, orientation and neighboring brightness; compare each candidate under both light directions. Use neutral light and color management consistently. Record what moved and what did not; change one factor at a time.\n**Failure Conditions and Side Effects:** A procedural node network or image texture is not evidence of improvement. Highlights and indirect light also depend on roughness, geometry and camera; light tests do not uniquely decompose appearance. Object-space mapping may distort on nonuniform scale; image coordinates may stretch or clamp on edges.\n**Evidence and Knowledge Origin:** Both historical material runs rendered color, image and roughness candidates under alternate lighting and retained image candidates. Procedural implementation here is an independently written general hypothesis, not their selected texture or a proven transfer.\n\n## Capability Name: Support and assembly diagnostics\n**Applicable Conditions:** An object may float, penetrate a support, or disconnect after a repair.\n**Problem and Technical Hypotheses:** Bounding boxes conflate support with proximity and do not show true contact. An assembly can be connected internally yet float, or rest on a floor while components are disconnected.\n**Executable Method:** `support.sample_support(obj, supports, tolerance, band, ray_margin)` samples evaluated mesh vertices in a low-Z band, casting vertically from above each sample onto the supplied evaluated mesh supports. Returns per-sample signed vertical gaps and a conservative category. World Z is up; supports must be explicitly supplied. A negative gap is only *possible* penetration. Missing rays yield unknown; near-zero is not proof of physical contact. `support.world_bounds(obj)` provides evaluated bounds for broad-phase checking only. Move a complete assembly or redesign its feet as separate candidates rather than blindly snapping individual parts.\n**Verification Method:** Inspect all feet/wheels, full sample coverage, local supports, side views, clearance, and changed silhouette; repeat after the selected repair. Separately inspect joints (wheel/fork, fork/spoke, post/seat), including top/side views and evaluated geometry. Use denser surface sampling or mesh intersection testing for critical results; rays and vertices alone can miss intersections, overhangs, or unsupported areas.\n**Failure Conditions and Side Effects:** Downward ray tests assume an appropriate horizontal support and do not establish stability, exact contact, connectivity, or volume intersection. The highest hit can be a top surface above the object; ray margin must exceed geometry thickness. Modifiers and sparse meshes affect sampling. Unknown is not a pass.\n**Evidence and Knowledge Origin:** Historical chair runs compared whole-chair lowering against caster changes with evaluated wheel samples and side views. Their numerical checks are sampled estimates, not a physics proof; prior knowledge supplies the interpretation boundaries.\n", 'numLines': 28, 'startLine': 1, 'totalLines': 28}}, 'origin': None}
2026-11-28 04:44:22,485 - INFO - [MainThread] - Received claude code message: {'content': [{'tool_use_id': 'call-d5b560cef70b47818e2f9a4bb65d287b', 'content': '1\t# Editable reconstruction capability library\n2\t\n3\tUse a single RGB as a set of constraints, not a ground-truth 3-D blueprint. Hidden geometry, absolute scale, light/material decomposition and camera/layout are underdetermined. Block the room and large objects first; use a consistent arbitrary scale and distinguish observation from hypotheses. Inspect major silhouettes, semantic facing, occlusion, vanishing directions, aspect ratios and object gaps before decorative details. Iterate camera and layout together: a plausible camera can make an incorrect layout look right, and vice versa. Preserve variants and compare fixed-camera renders before changing the view. No independent transfer improvement has been established.\n4\t\n5\tThe Python module is imported inside Blender (add this directory to `sys.path`). No function renders. `hinge_pose` changes the pivot transform; `directional_material` creates a new material. Other functions query the evaluated scene. Update the view layer after scene or camera changes before projection. `capabilities.json` is the index.\n6\t\n7\t## Capability Name: Joint camera/layout landmark fitting\n8\t**Applicable Conditions:** Visible room corners and object landmarks, especially wide-angle interiors. **Problem and Technical Hypotheses:** Framing, lens, positions, sizes and rotations interact. Image coordinates constrain projection but not unique depth. Observe front/back ordering and semantic face directions, not just point coordinates. **Executable Method:** `landmarks(scene,camera,points)` projects world-space points into normalized image coordinates, bottom-up; `landmark_error(projected,targets)` scores manually annotated targets `(x,y,weight)`. Start with room corners and major object endpoints, retain candidate cameras and layouts, adjust a small group at a time. Use occlusion and silhouette separately; error is not a complete image metric. **Verification Method:** render matched resolution and compare visible corners, overlap, negative space, object orientation, and crop; inspect an overhead view and multi-landmark changes. **Failure Conditions and Side Effects:** points outside frame, behind camera, hidden or wrongly paired can mislead; this function does not test occlusion. Joint fitting is non-unique. **Evidence and Knowledge Origin:** general projection knowledge; development camera transforms queried directly; historical camera adjustments are not evidence of independent improvement.\n9\t\n10\t## Capability Name: Hinged planar assemblies and occlusion\n11\t**Applicable Conditions:** Door, lid, or other hinged foreground surface. **Problem and Technical Hypotheses:** A missing face may be hidden by a solid wall, edge-on, outside view, or occluded by another object. A broad projected face alone does not establish a valid hinge. **Executable Method:** Build leaf, panels and handle as children of one pivot at a jamb. Leave pivot unparented; `hinge_pose(pivot, hinge_world, angle_radians)` swings its local geometry around that anchor; `hinge_error(pivot,jamb_point)` reports anchor displacement. Model a real opening in the wall, or assemble wall pieces around it. Hold camera fixed while testing swing, then test camera separately. **Verification Method:** compare camera outlines and overlap, diagnose with wall temporarily hidden, and inspect top and side views, hinge alignment, clearance, leaf-to-wall and furniture intersections. Restore visibility. **Failure Conditions and Side Effects:** anchor distance alone does not prove correct hinge orientation, wall opening or collision-free sweep; near-camera clipping and camera movement alter silhouette. Do not promote a visually attractive dislocated hinge to general repair. **Evidence and Knowledge Origin:** six-run exploration, especially door candidates and diagnostic views; observed limitations and rejected claims are detailed outside library.\n12\t\n13\t## Capability Name: Directional material and light separation\n14\t**Applicable Conditions:** Surfaces with directional grain, veining or mottling. **Problem and Technical Hypotheses:** Surface color pattern and illumination/highlight can look similar. Coordinate frame and anisotropic scale determine apparent pattern direction and frequency. **Executable Method:** `directional_material(name,scale,colors,roughness,coordinate)` creates a new editable Texture Coordinate → Mapping → Noise → ramp → base color network. Assign to chosen mesh slot; tune scale, colors and mapping in local coordinates. `Generated` depends on object bounds; `Object` uses object coordinates; `UV` requires a suitable UV map. Compare with plain-color roughness-only and, when appropriate, a separately authored image texture. Do not assume this noise is marble or reproduces the reference. **Verification Method:** render equal camera/exposure/lighting variants, compare surface crop, direction, contrast, edge stretching, and nearby objects. Repeat after moving light only: highlights move, fixed albedo features remain attached to surface, though illumination affects their visibility. **Failure Conditions and Side Effects:** generated coordinates stretch with aspect ratio, UV seams and side faces may distort, changes of light also alter shadows. Pattern complexity does not imply accuracy. **Evidence and Knowledge Origin:** Blender procedural mapping knowledge and the historical paired-light comparisons; direct GT node queries demonstrate various material bindings, not a unique ideal technique.\n15\t\n16\t## Capability Name: Support and connectivity screening\n17\t**Applicable Conditions:** Objects apparently resting on a floor or supported by another part. **Problem and Technical Hypotheses:** Floating, near contact, intersections, and disconnected assemblies are distinct. A global lowest point or AABB overlap does not verify all wheels or local supports. **Executable Method:** call `support_samples(object,support,tolerance,max_distance,sample_limit)` per explicitly selected support piece on evaluated mesh and surface. It samples lowest-band vertices and casts downward from slightly above; states are positive gap, near-zero, possible penetration or unknown support. Use `bounds_separation(a,b)` only as a broad-phase screen for potential connections, never as a contact test. Compare alternate whole-assembly translation versus localized geometry revisions, preserving pivots and seat clearance. **Verification Method:** inspect each intended contact in side views, cast or inspect additional relevant points, check for penetrations and separately inspect wheel–fork, fork–spoke, post–seat and other connections; rerun all tests after a repair. **Failure Conditions and Side Effects:** vertex sampling misses face interiors and non-lowest contacts, cannot reliably detect deep penetration, thin surfaces, overhangs, sparse meshes or unsupported regions. Ray hits are not force stability. Near-zero or AABB overlap is not proof of physical contact or assembly connectivity. Changing object heights can worsen silhouette and proportions. **Evidence and Knowledge Origin:** observed historical support tests and comparative chair edits; screening and uncertainty bounds are general technical knowledge.\n18\t', 'is_error': None}], 'uuid': '6acbe816-20a6-4b4a-abf1-c21b1004ab5b', 'parent_tool_use_id': None, 'tool_use_result': {'type': 'text', 'file': {'filePath': '/evidence/A5/public_library/SKILL.md', 'content': '# Editable reconstruction capability library\n\nUse a single RGB as a set of constraints, not a ground-truth 3-D blueprint. Hidden geometry, absolute scale, light/material decomposition and camera/layout are underdetermined. Block the room and large objects first; use a consistent arbitrary scale and distinguish observation from hypotheses. Inspect major silhouettes, semantic facing, occlusion, vanishing directions, aspect ratios and object gaps before decorative details. Iterate camera and layout together: a plausible camera can make an incorrect layout look right, and vice versa. Preserve variants and compare fixed-camera renders before changing the view. No independent transfer improvement has been established.\n\nThe Python module is imported inside Blender (add this directory to `sys.path`). No function renders. `hinge_pose` changes the pivot transform; `directional_material` creates a new material. Other functions query the evaluated scene. Update the view layer after scene or camera changes before projection. `capabilities.json` is the index.\n\n## Capability Name: Joint camera/layout landmark fitting\n**Applicable Conditions:** Visible room corners and object landmarks, especially wide-angle interiors. **Problem and Technical Hypotheses:** Framing, lens, positions, sizes and rotations interact. Image coordinates constrain projection but not unique depth. Observe front/back ordering and semantic face directions, not just point coordinates. **Executable Method:** `landmarks(scene,camera,points)` projects world-space points into normalized image coordinates, bottom-up; `landmark_error(projected,targets)` scores manually annotated targets `(x,y,weight)`. Start with room corners and major object endpoints, retain candidate cameras and layouts, adjust a small group at a time. Use occlusion and silhouette separately; error is not a complete image metric. **Verification Method:** render matched resolution and compare visible corners, overlap, negative space, object orientation, and crop; inspect an overhead view and multi-landmark changes. **Failure Conditions and Side Effects:** points outside frame, behind camera, hidden or wrongly paired can mislead; this function does not test occlusion. Joint fitting is non-unique. **Evidence and Knowledge Origin:** general projection knowledge; development camera transforms queried directly; historical camera adjustments are not evidence of independent improvement.\n\n## Capability Name: Hinged planar assemblies and occlusion\n**Applicable Conditions:** Door, lid, or other hinged foreground surface. **Problem and Technical Hypotheses:** A missing face may be hidden by a solid wall, edge-on, outside view, or occluded by another object. A broad projected face alone does not establish a valid hinge. **Executable Method:** Build leaf, panels and handle as children of one pivot at a jamb. Leave pivot unparented; `hinge_pose(pivot, hinge_world, angle_radians)` swings its local geometry around that anchor; `hinge_error(pivot,jamb_point)` reports anchor displacement. Model a real opening in the wall, or assemble wall pieces around it. Hold camera fixed while testing swing, then test camera separately. **Verification Method:** compare camera outlines and overlap, diagnose with wall temporarily hidden, and inspect top and side views, hinge alignment, clearance, leaf-to-wall and furniture intersections. Restore visibility. **Failure Conditions and Side Effects:** anchor distance alone does not prove correct hinge orientation, wall opening or collision-free sweep; near-camera clipping and camera movement alter silhouette. Do not promote a visually attractive dislocated hinge to general repair. **Evidence and Knowledge Origin:** six-run exploration, especially door candidates and diagnostic views; observed limitations and rejected claims are detailed outside library.\n\n## Capability Name: Directional material and light separation\n**Applicable Conditions:** Surfaces with directional grain, veining or mottling. **Problem and Technical Hypotheses:** Surface color pattern and illumination/highlight can look similar. Coordinate frame and anisotropic scale determine apparent pattern direction and frequency. **Executable Method:** `directional_material(name,scale,colors,roughness,coordinate)` creates a new editable Texture Coordinate → Mapping → Noise → ramp → base color network. Assign to chosen mesh slot; tune scale, colors and mapping in local coordinates. `Generated` depends on object bounds; `Object` uses object coordinates; `UV` requires a suitable UV map. Compare with plain-color roughness-only and, when appropriate, a separately authored image texture. Do not assume this noise is marble or reproduces the reference. **Verification Method:** render equal camera/exposure/lighting variants, compare surface crop, direction, contrast, edge stretching, and nearby objects. Repeat after moving light only: highlights move, fixed albedo features remain attached to surface, though illumination affects their visibility. **Failure Conditions and Side Effects:** generated coordinates stretch with aspect ratio, UV seams and side faces may distort, changes of light also alter shadows. Pattern complexity does not imply accuracy. **Evidence and Knowledge Origin:** Blender procedural mapping knowledge and the historical paired-light comparisons; direct GT node queries demonstrate various material bindings, not a unique ideal technique.\n\n## Capability Name: Support and connectivity screening\n**Applicable Conditions:** Objects apparently resting on a floor or supported by another part. **Problem and Technical Hypotheses:** Floating, near contact, intersections, and disconnected assemblies are distinct. A global lowest point or AABB overlap does not verify all wheels or local supports. **Executable Method:** call `support_samples(object,support,tolerance,max_distance,sample_limit)` per explicitly selected support piece on evaluated mesh and surface. It samples lowest-band vertices and casts downward from slightly above; states are positive gap, near-zero, possible penetration or unknown support. Use `bounds_separation(a,b)` only as a broad-phase screen for potential connections, never as a contact test. Compare alternate whole-assembly translation versus localized geometry revisions, preserving pivots and seat clearance. **Verification Method:** inspect each intended contact in side views, cast or inspect additional relevant points, check for penetrations and separately inspect wheel–fork, fork–spoke, post–seat and other connections; rerun all tests after a repair. **Failure Conditions and Side Effects:** vertex sampling misses face interiors and non-lowest contacts, cannot reliably detect deep penetration, thin surfaces, overhangs, sparse meshes or unsupported regions. Ray hits are not force stability. Near-zero or AABB overlap is not proof of physical contact or assembly connectivity. Changing object heights can worsen silhouette and proportions. **Evidence and Knowledge Origin:** observed historical support tests and comparative chair edits; screening and uncertainty bounds are general technical knowledge.\n', 'numLines': 18, 'startLine': 1, 'totalLines': 18}}, 'origin': None}
2026-11-28 04:44:22,490 - INFO - [MainThread] - Received claude code message: {'content': [{'tool_use_id': 'call-c3616a730b1e4e15acabeb5955678bb6', 'content': "1\t# Editable single-image reconstruction capability library\n2\t\n3\tStart with major image silhouettes and semantic relations, not support-gap optimization. Work at an arbitrary consistent scale: monocular absolute scale and hidden geometry are not identifiable. Keep an uncertainty list for off-frame and occluded parts. The helpers in `reconstruction.py` run inside Blender; import by adding this directory to `sys.path`. `capabilities.json` indexes callable entry points. Render candidate revisions and explicitly compare to the reference; numerical projections are supplementary, not an image-similarity oracle.\n4\t\n5\t## Capability Name: Joint camera, layout and occlusion\n6\t**Applicable Conditions:** Interior views with identifiable room edges, object corners or repeated parallel directions.  \n7\t**Problem and Technical Hypotheses:** Perspective, camera pose and layout interact: moving a chair or changing focal length can both change its projected position. Establish semantic fronts (chair opening, shelf face, cabinet doors) before polishing. Image-space occlusions constrain depth order but not unique distances.  \n8\t**Executable Method:** `aim_camera(camera, location, target, lens)` sets a perspective camera pose; `project_points(scene,camera,points)` maps world coordinates to normalized top-left image coordinates; `landmark_error(scene,camera,landmarks)` scores manually matched visible 3D points and image points. After dependency graph updates, alternate adjusting room envelope, camera lens/pose, then object footprints and heights. Match wall intersections, floor boundaries and large silhouettes first; separately check oriented fronts and which object covers which. Use several viewpoints in the viewport to prevent absurd hidden intersections. Preserve separate editable objects. Camera should not be parented during direct aiming unless transforms are handled by caller.  \n9\t**Verification Method:** Compare projected keypoints and rendered image overlay; inspect silhouettes and semantic orientation, and record occlusion order. Low landmark error does not guarantee correct camera or depth. Hold out some landmarks from tuning.  \n10\t**Failure Conditions and Side Effects:** Behind-camera landmarks raise an error; cropping, camera shift, distortion and occlusion need manual handling. Helpers mutate camera; camera/object joint solution is ambiguous.  \n11\t**Evidence and Knowledge Origin:** General projective geometry and teacher inference from reference images; direct GT inspection shows perspective cameras and transformed assemblies, not a unique solution. Transfer is untested.\n12\t\n13\t## Capability Name: Coherent hinged panels\n14\t**Applicable Conditions:** An open door or panel with an identifiable jamb and pivot.  \n15\t**Problem and Technical Hypotheses:** Placing a door only to match silhouette can detach the hinge or block an opening. A world-space pivot is a stronger structural constraint.  \n16\t**Executable Method:** `hinge_pose(door, hinge_world, angle_radians, local_hinge)` applies an incremental rotation about a world vertical axis and checks that the local hinge is already at the pivot. Make an actual wall opening and frame separately; choose plausible opening direction, then rotate the complete panel assembly, not just an ornament. For multi-object doors parent parts to one root first, preserving each child's world matrix while parenting and updating the dependency graph; otherwise parts may jump. Verify each part's world position before and after parenting and after rotation. Camera and wall must be checked after mutation.  \n17\t**Verification Method:** Project panel corners, compare visible outline; query hinge position before and after, inspect clearance from wall and frame in top view and render.  \n18\t**Failure Conditions and Side Effects:** Doesn't cut openings, detect collisions or infer hinge; wrong pivot raises error. Parent and constraint setups can change resulting transforms.  \n19\t**Evidence and Knowledge Origin:** General kinematics; GT door assets parented to door objects. This is not evidence of a particular repair being visually better.\n20\t\n21\t## Capability Name: Directional material and lighting separation\n22\t**Applicable Conditions:** Wood, brushed or directional surfaces with visible grain; a controllable light setup.  \n23\t**Problem and Technical Hypotheses:** Grain direction and scale depend on coordinates, transforms and mapping; shading gradients can be illumination rather than albedo. Avoid baking a lamp's glow into a surface color.  \n24\t**Executable Method:** `make_directional_material(name,color_a,color_b,axis_scale,noise_scale,roughness)` creates editable object-coordinate mapping, noise, ramp and Principled material and returns it; caller binds it to desired mesh faces. Colors are linear RGBA. Lower mapping scale along the intended grain direction stretches the pattern; choose coordinates relative to local axes, inspect rotated meshes and apply object scale or compensate mapping. For tile use separate parameterized planar coordinates and grid geometry or nodes; this helper is not a tile generator. First tune neutral base color and large lights, then roughness and subtle grain, evaluating under the final view transform.  \n25\t**Verification Method:** Render a test swatch under neutral illumination and an oblique view, then in scene; compare orientation, frequency, contrast and broad illumination separately. Temporarily disable pattern or change lighting to disambiguate shading.  \n26\t**Failure Conditions and Side Effects:** Stretched noise is not literal wood or metal and may streak incorrectly on different faces; object transforms affect mapping. New material increases datablocks; caller owns assignment and cleanup.  \n27\t**Evidence and Knowledge Origin:** General shader knowledge; direct scene inspection found object-coordinate mapping and procedural networks in development scenes, including a tile network, and varying UV availability. It does not establish this simplified material's visual superiority.\n28\t\n29\t## Capability Name: Support screening and assembly audit\n30\t**Applicable Conditions:** Suspected support pairs and multi-part editable objects.  \n31\t**Problem and Technical Hypotheses:** A floating-looking object may be supported by an unseen element. Bounds overlap does not demonstrate contact; parenting is logical connectivity, not structural support.  \n32\t**Executable Method:** `support_bounds(upper,lower,tolerance)` evaluates world AABBs, tests horizontal overlap, then reports signed vertical bound separation as positive gap, near-zero, possible penetration, or unknown support. `assembly_components(objects)` returns components connected by parent links only. Caller selects plausible pairs and tolerance in scene units; audit intended assembly hierarchy separately. For repair, edit only after visual/semantic inspection; re-evaluate all moved parts and render again.  \n33\t**Verification Method:** Inspect mesh surfaces or ray casts at multiple appropriate contact points, check support normals and projected footprint, then visually inspect alternate views and re-run the screen. Record unsupported unknowns rather than forcing contact.  \n34\t**Failure Conditions and Side Effects:** Rotated, hollow, concave and disconnected meshes can produce misleading bounds; AABB overlap and near-zero do not prove contact or support; negative gap is only possible penetration. Parent components do not detect loose mesh islands or constraints. Methods are read-only.  \n35\t**Evidence and Knowledge Origin:** General bounding-volume and scene-graph knowledge; direct Blender query found parent-child assemblies and mesh transforms. No claim of measured support improvement.\n36\t", 'is_error': None}], 'uuid': '79e1ee2b-fad4-4646-ba55-26722fd24b9d', 'parent_tool_use_id': None, 'tool_use_result': {'type': 'text', 'file': {'filePath': '/evidence/A4/public_library/SKILL.md', 'content': "# Editable single-image reconstruction capability library\n\nStart with major image silhouettes and semantic relations, not support-gap optimization. Work at an arbitrary consistent scale: monocular absolute scale and hidden geometry are not identifiable. Keep an uncertainty list for off-frame and occluded parts. The helpers in `reconstruction.py` run inside Blender; import by adding this directory to `sys.path`. `capabilities.json` indexes callable entry points. Render candidate revisions and explicitly compare to the reference; numerical projections are supplementary, not an image-similarity oracle.\n\n## Capability Name: Joint camera, layout and occlusion\n**Applicable Conditions:** Interior views with identifiable room edges, object corners or repeated parallel directions.  \n**Problem and Technical Hypotheses:** Perspective, camera pose and layout interact: moving a chair or changing focal length can both change its projected position. Establish semantic fronts (chair opening, shelf face, cabinet doors) before polishing. Image-space occlusions constrain depth order but not unique distances.  \n**Executable Method:** `aim_camera(camera, location, target, lens)` sets a perspective camera pose; `project_points(scene,camera,points)` maps world coordinates to normalized top-left image coordinates; `landmark_error(scene,camera,landmarks)` scores manually matched visible 3D points and image points. After dependency graph updates, alternate adjusting room envelope, camera lens/pose, then object footprints and heights. Match wall intersections, floor boundaries and large silhouettes first; separately check oriented fronts and which object covers which. Use several viewpoints in the viewport to prevent absurd hidden intersections. Preserve separate editable objects. Camera should not be parented during direct aiming unless transforms are handled by caller.  \n**Verification Method:** Compare projected keypoints and rendered image overlay; inspect silhouettes and semantic orientation, and record occlusion order. Low landmark error does not guarantee correct camera or depth. Hold out some landmarks from tuning.  \n**Failure Conditions and Side Effects:** Behind-camera landmarks raise an error; cropping, camera shift, distortion and occlusion need manual handling. Helpers mutate camera; camera/object joint solution is ambiguous.  \n**Evidence and Knowledge Origin:** General projective geometry and teacher inference from reference images; direct GT inspection shows perspective cameras and transformed assemblies, not a unique solution. Transfer is untested.\n\n## Capability Name: Coherent hinged panels\n**Applicable Conditions:** An open door or panel with an identifiable jamb and pivot.  \n**Problem and Technical Hypotheses:** Placing a door only to match silhouette can detach the hinge or block an opening. A world-space pivot is a stronger structural constraint.  \n**Executable Method:** `hinge_pose(door, hinge_world, angle_radians, local_hinge)` applies an incremental rotation about a world vertical axis and checks that the local hinge is already at the pivot. Make an actual wall opening and frame separately; choose plausible opening direction, then rotate the complete panel assembly, not just an ornament. For multi-object doors parent parts to one root first, preserving each child's world matrix while parenting and updating the dependency graph; otherwise parts may jump. Verify each part's world position before and after parenting and after rotation. Camera and wall must be checked after mutation.  \n**Verification Method:** Project panel corners, compare visible outline; query hinge position before and after, inspect clearance from wall and frame in top view and render.  \n**Failure Conditions and Side Effects:** Doesn't cut openings, detect collisions or infer hinge; wrong pivot raises error. Parent and constraint setups can change resulting transforms.  \n**Evidence and Knowledge Origin:** General kinematics; GT door assets parented to door objects. This is not evidence of a particular repair being visually better.\n\n## Capability Name: Directional material and lighting separation\n**Applicable Conditions:** Wood, brushed or directional surfaces with visible grain; a controllable light setup.  \n**Problem and Technical Hypotheses:** Grain direction and scale depend on coordinates, transforms and mapping; shading gradients can be illumination rather than albedo. Avoid baking a lamp's glow into a surface color.  \n**Executable Method:** `make_directional_material(name,color_a,color_b,axis_scale,noise_scale,roughness)` creates editable object-coordinate mapping, noise, ramp and Principled material and returns it; caller binds it to desired mesh faces. Colors are linear RGBA. Lower mapping scale along the intended grain direction stretches the pattern; choose coordinates relative to local axes, inspect rotated meshes and apply object scale or compensate mapping. For tile use separate parameterized planar coordinates and grid geometry or nodes; this helper is not a tile generator. First tune neutral base color and large lights, then roughness and subtle grain, evaluating under the final view transform.  \n**Verification Method:** Render a test swatch under neutral illumination and an oblique view, then in scene; compare orientation, frequency, contrast and broad illumination separately. Temporarily disable pattern or change lighting to disambiguate shading.  \n**Failure Conditions and Side Effects:** Stretched noise is not literal wood or metal and may streak incorrectly on different faces; object transforms affect mapping. New material increases datablocks; caller owns assignment and cleanup.  \n**Evidence and Knowledge Origin:** General shader knowledge; direct scene inspection found object-coordinate mapping and procedural networks in development scenes, including a tile network, and varying UV availability. It does not establish this simplified material's visual superiority.\n\n## Capability Name: Support screening and assembly audit\n**Applicable Conditions:** Suspected support pairs and multi-part editable objects.  \n**Problem and Technical Hypotheses:** A floating-looking object may be supported by an unseen element. Bounds overlap does not demonstrate contact; parenting is logical connectivity, not structural support.  \n**Executable Method:** `support_bounds(upper,lower,tolerance)` evaluates world AABBs, tests horizontal overlap, then reports signed vertical bound separation as positive gap, near-zero, possible penetration, or unknown support. `assembly_components(objects)` returns components connected by parent links only. Caller selects plausible pairs and tolerance in scene units; audit intended assembly hierarchy separately. For repair, edit only after visual/semantic inspection; re-evaluate all moved parts and render again.  \n**Verification Method:** Inspect mesh surfaces or ray casts at multiple appropriate contact points, check support normals and projected footprint, then visually inspect alternate views and re-run the screen. Record unsupported unknowns rather than forcing contact.  \n**Failure Conditions and Side Effects:** Rotated, hollow, concave and disconnected meshes can produce misleading bounds; AABB overlap and near-zero do not prove contact or support; negative gap is only possible penetration. Parent components do not detect loose mesh islands or constraints. Methods are read-only.  \n**Evidence and Knowledge Origin:** General bounding-volume and scene-graph knowledge; direct Blender query found parent-child assemblies and mesh transforms. No claim of measured support improvement.\n", 'numLines': 36, 'startLine': 1, 'totalLines': 36}}, 'origin': None}
